  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Warmer than average temperatures again this week.\n\nEnjoy the conditions today as we can expect another warm period\nfor this week. Similar to the start of September, a large mid-\nlevel ridge over the Great Plains will move eastward and bring\nwarmer and humid conditions to the southeastern CONUS and the \nMid-Atlantic. The good news is that it won't be nearly as hot \nand Heat Advisories are very unlikely. The bad news is it will \nstill be unseasonably warm with high temperatures in the mid-80s\nto mid-90s by Thursday afternoon. Thursday is currently forecast\nto be the warmest of the week and the high temperatures will \ngradually rise each day until then. A cold front is forecast to \napproach the region towards this weekend which may be able to \nbring temperatures down a little.\n\nKey Message 2: Another round of showers and thunderstorms\npossible later in the week.\n\nA surface high pressure system will keep conditions dry for the\nfirst half of this work week before a cold front brings another\nround of precipitation by Thursday. The timing of the front's \npassage is a little uncertain and adds a monkey wrench into the \nforecast. Since Thursday will be the warmest of the week, it may\nbe the most unstable and the front's passage could coincide \nwith favorable timing for severe weather. The front also looks \nto bring favorable wind shear conditions (25-35 knots) as its \nassociated 500mb trough moves through the region. Model guidance\nhas the southeastern ridge breaking up into two small pieces \nwith one remaining over the southeast and the other in the \nsouthwest CONUS. This results in a trough covering the gap \nbetween the mid-level highs and some models have vorticity and a\nsmall shortwave making its way around the southeastern ridge \nand northeastward towards the Mid-Altantic. This too will have \ndownstream effects on which parts of the forecast region will \nreceive precipitation and could delay the front's passage. For \nthe time being, the severe threat looks to be low with \nprecipitation chances possible Thursday through Sunday. One \nelement of the forecast that has greater confidence is the \nincrease of moisture and PWATs by the weekend. With PWAT values \napproaching into the 1.50-2.00 inch range, the concern for heavy\nrainfall rates and flooding is present during the front's \npassage. So far, the upslope nature of the storms that will \nlikely accompany the front place the mountains of WV and \nsouthwest VA at the greatest risk. Nevertheless, because of the \ntiming conditions, this forecast will likely need several \nupdates until the event's arrival.",
